oney": 1.13}, "rating_margin": 20.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.13, "baseline_margin": 21.42, "analyst_adjustment": 0.08, "key_factors": ["D+11 district with a well-funded incumbent seeking another term", "September UNH poll shows Pingree up 22 among likely voters", "Russell is a repeat longshot challenger with minimal cash ($20K on hand)", "Prediction markets price Pingree near 96%"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Safe D race: an incumbent in a D+11 seat, a September UNH poll showing a 22-point lead, and a challenger running his second longshot campaign with almost no money. Markets and the polling agree, so no adjustment is warranted.", "watch": "A major late-breaking shift in the national political environment or a strong Russell debate performance could narrow the margin, though a real upset remains unlikely.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Incumbent Chellie Pingree (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Chellie Pingree seeks another term in Maine's 1st District, a seat that leans Democratic by about 11 points. She faces Ronald Russell, a Republican running his second longshot race against her, in a single debate days before the election. Pingree has out-raised Russell roughly six to one and holds a large cash advantage, with no meaningful outside spending against her.\n\nA University of New Hampshire poll of likely voters in late September showed Pingree ahead 57 to 35, and prediction markets price her win probability around 96 percent. With the incumbent's advantage, the district's lean, and the polling all pointing the same direction, the race sits firmly in Safe D territory heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.989, "margin": 20.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9938, "margin": 20.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9879, "margin": 20.9}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9946, "margin": 21.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "